1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,220
So I'm keeping an open mind.

2
00:00:01,740 --> 00:00:04,880
I think when you build an observatory

3
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:07,920
that has profoundly new capabilities,

4
00:00:09,180 --> 00:00:11,420
the most exciting science is going to be the thing

5
00:00:11,420 --> 00:00:12,220
that you didn't expect.

6
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,800
So you want to approach what you're going to do

7
00:00:15,800 --> 00:00:17,960
with the observatory and how you're going to use it

8
00:00:17,960 --> 00:00:19,060
with something of an open mind

9
00:00:19,060 --> 00:00:24,000
so that we can pivot to following the surprises,

10
00:00:24,260 --> 00:00:27,580
following the things that are most impactful.

11
00:00:28,180 --> 00:00:30,920
With Roman, we're discovering space on steroids.

12
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:33,060
Because we do large surveys,

13
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,580
we're not picking a galaxy and going to study that galaxy.

14
00:00:36,680 --> 00:00:38,540
We're not picking a star to go and look at that star.

15
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:41,060
We're mapping out large regions of the sky

16
00:00:41,060 --> 00:00:42,960
with extraordinary sensitivity.

17
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,180
And we're going to find all the things that are there.

18
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:46,860
So we're going to study the things that we know about

19
00:00:46,860 --> 00:00:48,040
and we're going to find the things

20
00:00:48,040 --> 00:00:49,980
we didn't even know to look for.

